 apple fire ignited near the  cherry valley community  the wildfire started on friday evening  and has scorched nearly  800 hectares since it began so far  no injuries have been reported the blaze  is one of several fires being fought  around southern california  as the region deals with a  record-breaking heat wave  amid natural disasters and a pandemic  the u.s president is waging a new war  with china over a video sharing app  donald trump is threatening to ban  tick-tock from the u.s  up to 80 million americans use the  chinese-owned app  per month tick-tock has denied  accusations that it's controlled by  or shares data with the chinese  government trump hasn't said when  or how he'll enforce the ban  breakthrough therapy is still ahead the  new drug therapies in canada for covid19  it's been five months since canada  reported its first case of cobit 19.  we're far from a cure but potential  treatments have been developed at record  speeds  heather yorick's west looks at the  promising drug therapies underway  across the country in the race to find  new therapies to fight the novel  coronavirus  one of the biggest breakthroughs came  when scientists in england discover that  a cheap and readily available steroid  drug  could effectively treat patients with  covid19 prior to the last few weeks you  might have only had maybe about 50  percent of doctors that were using  steroids in patients with  severe covet lung injury now i'd say  almost 100 percent of doctors are using  steroids in this population  dexamethasone doesn't target the virus  itself  rather it works to treat inflammation  caused by covid19  in a large clinical trial the drug was  found to help prevent one  death in every eight coven 19 patients  on ventilators  so the dexamethasone right now is  the therapy that i would say would be  leading in terms of its  benefit to patients and then the next  one that i mentioned was the rem  deserver which is the drugs  that specifically targets the  coronavirus itself  the anti-viral drug has shown promise in  clinical trials both in shortening the  duration of illness and reducing  mortality  but the drug was only recently approved  for use in canada  at the beginning of the pandemic you  know we were using it on a special  access  permit but it took many days to obtain  so it really wasn't practical  meanwhile another therapy that received  considerable buzz  early in the pandemic thanks to  endorsements from the u.s president  failed to deliver results when examined  in clinical trials  i think everybody is pretty convinced  that hydroxychloroquine does not work  for kova 19 and uh we were never using  it  here outside of clinical trials but  nowhere  i think is using that even anecdotally  now of course none of the new therapies  are considered cures by any stretch  but when compared to what was available  to physicians just  six months ago it's clear that in a very  short period of time  the way this new illness can be treated  has come a long way  heather urex west global news calgary  up next the self-described geek who  thinks this robot is the answer to those  dreaded zoom calls  the flag's all yours it's been a  pressure  astronauts doug hurley and bob bankin  were awarded one of the international  space station's  american flags today as they prepare to  leave the station  ending an historic mission by spacex two  months ago the astronauts held the first  ever launch of a manned  private spacecraft as well as the first  manned launch from u.s soil  in nearly a decade they're expected to  be back on earth  tomorrow the pandemic has forced  all of us to find new ways to stay  connected with loved ones  but a skype or zoom call isn't quite the  same as a chat  face to face and that got one british  toy designer thinking  as redmond shannon explains he's come up  with a cheap and safe way to transport  yourself  into someone else's home  ross atkin is a proud geek a design  consultant who's never stopped being the  kid  obsessed by lego yeah i feel like the  first job i got in design i was like  really they're going to pay me to  design stuff and you know build models  and do cad and  illustrator and stuff like because i was  like i'd just do that you know  i'd do that i didn't do that anyway he's  been designing self-assembly robot toys  for five years  and when the whole world started zooming  each other during the pandemic  a thought occurred to him having a video  call is nothing like  being with someone because you're stuck  in this little  rectangle and you're completely  dependent on what they're doing with  their rectangle  so atkin very quickly designed this  a cardboard telepresence robot  using a simple smartphone app  large telepresence robots for offices  can cost  thousands of dollars atkins model funded  by a kickstarter campaign  costs about a hundred bucks and is more  about engaging  small kids than big clients being able  to just  leave um the grandparents or the in-laws  in the robot  to chase the toddler around themselves  without having to  follow him around holding the phone that  then meant both of us could do something  he could play and they could still  interact with him it's not a substitute  for babysitting but it does add a new  level of  interaction for kids of all sizes even  news reporters too  although i could have used a rear camera  atkin also designs products for people  with disabilities  he thinks larger versions could have  many applications beyond the pandemic  you can make some care tasks  a lot more convenient for the carer and  for the person being cared for  and you can end up with the carer being  able to spend more time caring for  the person because they aren't having to  travel being a tiny robot  is not quite face to face contact but as  we've all learned in 2020  technology and clever design can help us  feel that little bit  closer to our loved ones redmond shannon  global news  london and that is global national for  thi